<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sinusoidal_waves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sinusoidal Waves</title>
			
  	
<p>
	Waves can take any shape or size, and do not necessarily have a regular, smooth, repeating pattern. However, if a <em> wave source </em> oscillates with simple harmonic motion, then the wave that is generated will be a <alert>sinusoidal wave</alert> (more specifically, a <em>traveling</em> sinusoidal wave).  Sinusoidal waves are periodic in both <em> space </em> and <em> time </em>, so the displacement of a particle in a medium is symbolized by a function like <m> y(x,t) </m> or <m> D(x,t) </m>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-sinusoidal-wave">
	<title>Sinusoidal Wave</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A sinusoidal wave with amplitude <m>y_{\mathrm{max}}</m>, wavelength <m>\lambda</m>, period <m>T</m>, and initial phase <m>\phi_i</m> can be written
			<me> y(x,t) = y_{\mathrm{max}} \sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}x \pm \frac{2\pi}{T}t + \phi_i\right)</me>
			Alternatively, the wave may be written in terms of the wavenumber <m>k = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}</m> and the angular frequency <m>\omega = \frac{2\pi}{T}</m>:
			<me> y(x,t) = y_{\mathrm{max}} \sin\left(kx \pm \omega t + \phi_i\right)</me>
			The sign of the <m>\pm</m> determines the direction of travel: choosing <m>-</m> corresponds to a wave moving in the <alert>positive</alert> direction, while choosing <m>+</m> corresponds to a wave moving in the <alert>negative</alert> direction.
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

	<definition xml:id="def_phase">
	<title>Initial Phase</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>The <term>phase</term> of a wave, typically written as <m>\phi</m>, refers to where in a cycle (from <m>0</m> to <m>2\pi</m>) a wave is at any given point in time and space.  
		
		The <term>initial phase</term>, <m>\phi_i</m>, specifies the phase at the origin at <m>t = 0</m>.</p>
	</statement>
</definition>


<subsubsection>
<title>Activities</title>
	<activity xml:id="x_trig-waves">
	<title>Graphing Phase</title>
	<p>
		
	</p>
	<p>Use a graphing program (like <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator" >Desmos</url> or <url href="https://www.wolframalpha.com/">Mathematica</url>), to graph the following functions (use <m>\lambda = 6 \mathrm{~m}</m>).</p>
				<p>
				<ul>
				<li><m> \sin{(2\pi\frac{x}{\lambda})} </m> </li>
				<li><m> \sin{(2\pi\frac{x}{\lambda} + \frac{\pi}{3})}</m></li>
				<li><m> \sin{(2\pi\frac{x}{\lambda} + \frac{\pi}{6})}</m></li>
				<li><m>\sin{(2\pi\frac{x}{\lambda} - \frac{\pi}{3})}</m></li>
				</ul>
				</p>
			</activity>
			<activity>
			<title>Exploring Phase</title>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>Based on your graphs, what is the mathematical role of the phase in an oscillation or wave equation?  What do you think the physical role of the phase is?</p>
			</activity>
		</subsubsection>
		
